{"id":"workloads-autoscaling-vertical-pod-autoscale","corpus":"k8s","title":"Vertical Pod Autoscaling","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/autoscaling/vertical-pod-autoscale/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Vertical Pod Autoscaling"},{"kind":"p","text":"In Kubernetes, a VerticalPodAutoscaler automatically updates a workload management resource (such as a Deployment or StatefulSet), with the aim of automatically adjusting infrastructure resource requests and limits to match actual usage."},{"kind":"p","text":"Vertical scaling means that the response to increased resource demand is to assign more resources (for example: memory or CPU) to the Pods that are already running for the workload. This is also known as rightsizing, or sometimes autopilot. This is different from horizontal scaling, which for Kubernetes would mean deploying more Pods to distribute the load."},{"kind":"p","text":"If the resource usage decreases, and the Pod resource requests are above optimal levels, the VerticalPodAutoscaler instructs the workload resource (the Deployment, StatefulSet, or other similar resource) to adjust resource requests back down, preventing resource waste."},{"kind":"p","text":"The VerticalPodAutoscaler is implemented as a Kubernetes API resource and a controller. The resource determines the behavior of the controller. The vertical pod autoscaling controller, running within the Kubernetes data plane, periodically adjusts the resource requests and limits of its target (for example, a Deployment) based on analysis of historical resource utilization, the amount of resources available in the cluster, and real-time events such as out-of-memory (OOM) conditions."},{"kind":"h2","text":"API object"},{"kind":"p","text":"The VerticalPodAutoscaler is defined as a Custom Resource Definition (CRD) in Kubernetes. Unlike HorizontalPodAutoscaler, which is part of the core Kubernetes API, VPA must be installed separately in your cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"The current stable API version is autoscaling.k8s.io/v1. More details about the VPA installation and API can be found in the VPA GitHub repository."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How does a VerticalPodAutoscaler work?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes implements vertical pod autoscaling through multiple cooperating components that run intermittently (it is not a continuous process). The VPA consists of three main components:"},{"kind":"li","text":"The recommender, which analyzes resource usage and provides recommendations."},{"kind":"li","text":"The updater, that Pod resource requests either by evicting Pods or modifying them in place."},{"kind":"li","text":"And the VPA admission controller webhook, which applies resource recommendations to new or recreated Pods."},{"kind":"p","text":"Once during each period, the Recommender queries the resource utilization for Pods targeted by each VerticalPodAutoscaler definition. The Recommender finds the target resource defined by the targetRef, then selects the pods based on the target resource's .spec.selector labels, and obtains the metrics from the resource metrics API to analyze actual CPU and memory consumption."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Recommender analyzes both current and historical resource usage data (CPU and memory) for each Pod targeted by the VerticalPodAutoscaler. It examines:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Historical consumption patterns over time to identify trends"},{"kind":"li","text":"Peak usage and variance to ensure sufficient headroom"},{"kind":"li","text":"Out-of-memory (OOM) events and other resource-related incidents"},{"kind":"p","text":"Based on this analysis, the Recommender calculates three types of recommendations:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Target recommendation (optimal resources for typical usage)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Lower bound (minimum viable resources)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Upper bound (maximum reasonable resources)."},{"kind":"p","text":"These recommendations are stored in the VerticalPodAutoscaler resource's .status.recommendation field."},{"kind":"p","text":"The updater component monitors the VerticalPodAutoscaler resources and compares current Pod resource requests with the recommendations. When the difference exceeds configured thresholds and the update policy allows it, the updater can either:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Evict Pods, triggering their recreation with new resource requests (traditional approach)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Update Pod resources in place without eviction, when the cluster supports in-place Pod resource updates"},{"kind":"p","text":"The chosen method depends on the configured update mode, cluster capabilities, and the type of resource change needed. In-place updates, when available, avoid Pod disruption but may have limitations on which resources can be modified. The updater respects PodDisruptionBudgets to minimize service impact."},{"kind":"p","text":"The admission controller operates as a mutating webhook that intercepts Pod creation requests. It checks if the Pod is targeted by a VerticalPodAutoscaler and, if so, applies the recommended resource requests and limits before the Pod is created. More specifically, the admission controller uses the Target recommendation in the VerticalPodAutoscaler resource's .status.recommendation stanza as the new resource requests. The admission controller ensures new Pods start with appropriately sized resource allocations, whether they're created during initial deployment, after an eviction by the updater, or due to scaling operations."},{"kind":"p","text":"The VerticalPodAutoscaler requires a metrics source, such as Kubernetes' Metrics Server add-on, to be installed in the cluster. The VPA components fetch metrics from the metrics.k8s.io API. The Metrics Server needs to be launched separately as it is not deployed by default in most clusters. For more information about resource metrics, see Metrics Server."}],"lemmas":{"verticals":["vertical","verticals"],"autoscalings":["autoscalings","autoscaling"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"automaticallies":["automaticallies","automatically"],"updates":["update","updates"],"updating":["updating","update"],"updated":["updated","update"],"workloads":["workload","workloads"],"managements":["management","managements"],"deployments":["deployment","deployments"],"aims":["aim","aims"],"aimed":["aimed","aim"],"aiming":["aiming","aim"],"adjusting":["adjusting","adjust"],"adjustings":["adjustings","adjusting"],"adjusted":["adjusted","adjust"],"infrastructures":["infrastructure","infrastructures"],"requests":["request","requests"],"requesting":["requesting","request"],"requested":["requested","request"],"limits":["limit","limits"],"limited":["limited","limit"],"limiting":["limiting","limit"],"matches":["match","matches"],"matched":["matched","match"],"matching":["matching","match"],"actuals":["actuals","actual"],"usages":["usage","usages"],"scaling":["scaling","scale"],"scalings":["scaling","scalings"],"scales":["scale","scales"],"scaled":["scaled","scale"],"means":["mean","means"],"meanes":["meanes","mean","meane"],"meaning":["meaning","mean"],"meant":["meant","mean"],"responses":["response","responses"],"increased":["increased","increase"],"increaseds":["increaseds","increased"],"increases":["increase","increases"],"increasing":["increasing","increase"],"demands":["demand","demands"],"demanded":["demanded","demand"],"demanding":["demanding","demand"],"assigns":["assigns","assign"],"assigned":["assigned","assign"],"assigning":["assigning","assign"],"resources":["resource","resources"],"examples":["example","examples"],"memories":["memory","memories"],"pods":["pod","pods"],"alreadies":["alreadies","already"],"running":["running","run"],"runnings":["running","runnings"],"runs":["run","runs"],"ran":["ran","run"],"known":["known","know"],"knowns":["knowns","known"],"knows":["know","knows"],"knowed":["k